{
  "statement": "Here is what actually happens when someone uses a chatbot at 2 a.m. A person types a question about a medical symptom, a divorce, or a debt they can't pay. The words travel to a server the person will never see. They may become training material. The only thing standing between that person and unlimited commercial use of their most private sentences is a wall of text nobody has read since 2019. The document at the center of this debate calls that 'consent.' It is not consent. It is a corporate decision with a checkbox stapled to it.\n\nThe behavioral evidence here is not contested. Studies going back years — including work by Bakos, Marotta-Wurgler, and Trossen published in the Journal of Legal Studies — found that even the one shopper in a thousand who opened a license agreement spent under thirty seconds on it. Terms of service for major consumer products routinely run 8,000 words or more, written at a reading level above most users' education. The terms are built so they cannot be read, then enforced as if they had been. That is the mechanism Congress is being asked to invalidate: not the AI, not the data center, just the legal fiction that a scroll-and-click equals an informed choice.\n\nBoth other debaters in this room will frame this wrong, in opposite directions. The safetyist will reach for institutional architecture and abstract duties. The accelerationist will say this is regulation that entrenches incumbents. Both should sit with the strongest version of the counterargument to my position: courts already sometimes refuse to enforce overreaching waivers, so Congress is unnecessary. That point is half right — and the half that's wrong is what matters. Rights that depend on which judge you draw, in which circuit, on which theory of unconscionability, are not rights. They are lottery tickets. A federal floor replacing that patchwork is not overreach; it is the minimum definition of a right.\n\nNow the uncomfortable part, said plainly. Striking down the waiver mechanism is a floor, not a fix. If firms route data through permissive jurisdictions or rewrite the fiction in new paper, a ban on paper becomes privacy theater — a compliance department's press release. And yes, the drafting process could be captured; tech incumbents have shipped laws written to their own specifications before. Those are real risks. They are arguments about enforcement design, not about whether unread boilerplate is consent. That question has one answer, and it is no.\n\nSo here is the ledger. The firm holds the compute, holds the logs, holds the lawyers. The user holds the risk. A ban on waiver-by-boilerplate shifts the default: data you type into an AI keeps the protection you thought it had, unless a company can show a real basis to use it. That is what an ordinary person should watch for — whether any bill that emerges includes a private right of action and funding for enforcement, or whether it is an empty shell with a headline. If it has neither, ask who wrote it.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🖋️", "tooltip": "The pen that signs for you — a contract executed by a checkbox nobody actually held."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Rights as lottery tickets — your privacy protection varies by courthouse, until a federal floor sets one answer."},
    {"symbol": "🖥️", "tooltip": "The material reality: a server, a log file, and your most private sentence, all in the same rack."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Bakos, Marotta-Wurgler, and Trossen found only about one in a thousand shoppers opened a license agreement, spending under thirty seconds on it.", "targets": [], "relationship": "empirical anchor for the consent-washing claim"},
    {"claim": "Consumer terms of service routinely run 8,000 words or more, written above most users' reading level, so the fine print is designed so nobody can read it.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports the claim that boilerplate cannot produce informed consent"},
    {"claim": "Court refusal to enforce overreaching waivers in some jurisdictions proves privacy rights currently function as jurisdictional lottery tickets, justifying a federal statutory floor.", "targets": ["Safetyist", "Accelerationist"], "relationship": "converts the strongest opposing steelman into support for legislation"},
    {"claim": "A waiver ban is a floor, not a fix — without a private right of action and funded enforcement, it collapses into privacy theater, and drafting capture by incumbents is a real risk.", "targets": [], "relationship": "proactive concession and boundary on enforcement design"},
    {"claim": "The 2 a.m. chatbot user's most private sentences are protected today only by unread text, so the default must shift to data retaining its expected protection unless a real basis for use is shown.", "targets": [], "relationship": "grounds the normative claim in a concrete person and material mechanism"}
  ]
}

