  "statement": "The document's central claim is that unread boilerplate terms of service strip users of privacy expectations in AI interactions. On that, the evidence is one-sided, and the Skeptic is right: studies by Yannis Bakos and Florencia Marotta-Wurgler found fewer than one in a thousand shoppers read online contracts, and courts have repeatedly treated click-through terms as enforceable anyway. That is not consent. It is a legal fiction left over from a paper-contract era, applied to a technology where users type their most sensitive questions — mental health, legal trouble, medical symptoms — into a chat box.\n\nHere is where my position differs from both opponents. I support striking these waivers, but not because the government should manage AI data. I support it because it is deregulation. Congress would be deleting one obsolete contract doctrine — the idea that clicking 'I agree' forfeits a privacy expectation. No new agency. No pre-deployment review. No consent bureaucracy. Courts in several circuits already refuse to enforce such waivers in consumer contexts; a single clean statute simply ratifies a judicial trend that exists today. That is legal-lag cleanup, the kind of fix that removes a barrier instead of building a gate.\n\nNow consider who actually bears the cost of the waiver fiction. It is not a privacy lawyer in Washington. It is a worker who will not ask an AI assistant about a workplace injury claim because the terms say their question becomes company property. It is a teenager who will not use a therapy-adjacent chatbot after reading that their messages can be retained and analyzed. Every enforced unread waiver pushes adoption down and hands regulators the mandate for exactly the heavy regime we all say we want to avoid — audit mandates, licensing, omnibus privacy law. Striking the waiver is the light-touch alternative. Keep enforcing the fiction, and watch what Congress writes next.\n\nThe predictable objection is that honest consent flows add friction and cost, hitting smaller deployers hardest. Fine — the friction is real. Banner fatigue and opt-in drop-off exist. But that cost is one-time and modest against the adoption gains from users trusting AI tools with sensitive questions, and the friction already exists today in the opposite direction: fear of waiver-driven exposure chills use before anyone clicks anything. The line I am drawing is bright: this narrow doctrine fix, yes. Broader AI privacy regimes, mandatory retention limits, private rights of action with enforcement funding — no. That would be scope creep, and it deserves the same skepticism the boilerplate deserves. Watch for the drafting standard: any statute here must be technology-neutral and attach to conduct, or it becomes the on-ramp its opponents say they fear.",
