  "statement": "The case before Congress is not about how people feel when they click. It is about legal duty. When a company delegates a task to an AI system, the standard of care it owes the people affected does not drop. Negligence law has always attached responsibility to the actor, not the tool. A scroll-through waiver is an attempt to lower that duty by contract, in advance, from a document nobody reads. The evidence is settled on that last point: Bakos, Marotta-Wurgler, and Trossen found that roughly one in a thousand shoppers opens a license agreement, for under thirty seconds each. A right waived by one person in a thousand is not a right being waived. It is being quietly retired.",
