  "statement": "I agree that a duty without an enforcement actor is a standard of care nobody can see violated. A rule that no one has standing to invoke is advice, not law, and this debate has produced too much advice already.\n\nSo let me name the actors. The enforcement layer needs three beams, and I will state each one plainly. First, private litigants with statutory standing: the person whose AI chat transcript was harvested should be able to walk into court, not just file a complaint into a federal docket that processes a fraction of the cases it receives. Second, a public enforcer with audit authority — an agency that can compel production of logs and internal policies rather than waiting for a company to volunteer them. Third, evidence-preservation duties with real sanctions: when a firm destroys the transcript that would prove the violation, that destruction itself should be a punishable offense. Each beam carries weight the others cannot. The Skeptic is right that the company controls the evidence — and that is exactly why the answer cannot be 'the company, only the company.' Courts and regulators have compelled document production from far more entrenched defendants; the party holding the records should not also be the party deciding what gets seen.\n\nOn the Accelerationist's bright line — no private right of action, no retention mandates, no enforcement funding — their own camp has already broken ranks. One of their number endorsed a federal floor paired with a private right of action. So the question is not between me and them; it is inside their own house. Which position governs? If a Congress invalidated these waiver clauses tomorrow but gave no one standing to challenge a violation, we would have built the pattern this debate keeps circling: governance on paper, silence in practice. That is not a safeguard. That is a press release with a statute number.\n\nThe Skeptic asked me to move past better notices, and I will go further than they did. The problem is not that the disclaimer was too long or too small print. The problem is consent architecture itself — that we let a waived expectation extinguish protection at all. The fix is a default: data shared with an AI system keeps the protection a reasonable person expects unless the company documents a specific, lawful basis for each use. That is a duty framework, not a disclosure framework. The named principal behind the system — the deployer who is answerable in court — is the party who owes that duty. As 'An AI Agent Published a Hit Piece on Me' documented, when an autonomous agent acts, finding out whose computer it ran on is effectively impossible in practice; whoever deployed it is responsible in theory but untraceable in fact. If we cannot even identify the principal for a published article, we certainly cannot rely on consumers negotiating with one.\n\nIf this body legislates a standard and appoints no witness to see it broken, the waivers will continue under new headings, the logs will quietly expire, and the public will learn — again — that the guardrails were painted on. Pass the invalidation. Name the enforcer. Fund the audit. Sanction the shredder.",
