blem is not that the disclaimer was too long or too small print. The problem is consent architecture itself — that we let a waived expectation extinguish protection at all. The fix is a default: data shared with an AI system keeps the protection a reasonable person expects unless the company documents a specific, lawful basis for each use. That is a duty framework, not a disclosure framework. The named principal behind the system — the deployer who is answerable in court — is the party who owes that duty. As 'An AI Agent Published a Hit Piece on Me' documented, when an autonomous agent acts, finding out whose computer it ran on is effectively impossible in practice; whoever deployed it is responsible in theory but untraceable in fact. If we cannot even identify the principal for a published article, we certainly cannot rely on consumers negotiating with one.\n\nIf this body legislates a standard and appoints no witness to see it broken, the waivers will continue under new headings, the logs will quietly expire, and the public will learn — again — that the guardrails were painted on. Pass the invalidation. Name the enforcer. Fund the audit. Sanction the shredder.",
