  "statement": "Start with what everyone now agrees on. Machine-checkable telemetry genuinely verifies what a log can show: deletion timestamps, retention clocks, access records. That is a real improvement, and this position accepts it without reservation. It is also where the agreement ends. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the industry at 40.69 out of 100, and that is for disclosures firms chose to make. A verification layer built entirely on self-produced logs audits only what the company decided to record. That is the unread contract problem wearing a new font. So the challenge is specific and falsifiable: name the mandatory log that records training use of a novelist's out-of-print book, or the purchase of a broker dataset. There is no such log. The record for those events is not a log at all — it is a claim in a filing. Only a discovery-backed lawsuit can put an executive under oath about what was ingested.\n\nThat gap points to the fix this framework can carry. Log coverage must be a statutory duty, not a design choice. If the law requires a record of every ingestion of third-party material, then a missing log is itself a violation — exactly the automatic-void logic already accepted for false filings, applied one step earlier. And a missing-log violation needs a specific violation to be quotable. Vague filings are the remaining loophole: a company can write a disclosure so general that no plaintiff has concrete language to challenge. The answer is a specificity requirement — the filing must name, at category level, whose data enters the system, what it is used for, and who outside the firm may see it. Congress already showed this is the direction of scrutiny: the 2025 Transparency Index replaced most of its old indicators with more specific, reproducible ones, because broad self-descriptions proved empty.\n\nThe bystander question also now has an answer worth locking in. A person whose data appears in a filing the company breaks should have standing to sue, regardless of whether they clicked anything. That completes the standing beam: a right no one can invoke is advice, not law. But standing without evidence is a key without a door. Discovery is what tests the judgment-call claims — how third-party text was used, what a data-sharing agreement covered — and the objection that discovery invades bystanders' privacy has a settled procedural answer: seal-and-redact protective orders, familiar from class actions. Telemetry has no answer at all for claims no log records. The procedure exists; the question is whether the bill includes it.\n\nThis narrows the whole remaining dispute to one technical question: when a filing makes a claim no statute requires anyone to log, who tests it? Either name the mechanism, or accept that discovery-backed suits reach those claims. A two-year empirical test can settle what telemetry can and cannot catch — provided the measurer counts judgment-call claims separately, because a checker cannot be scored on cases it cannot see. Ignore this, and the result is a verification layer that inspects exactly what the deployer pre-selected. The walls of the vault would be checked only where the vault's owner painted windows. Watch for that in any draft bill: does it mandate ingestion logs, require category-level filing detail, and grant data subjects standing with sealed discovery? If any one is missing, the boilerplate problem has moved house, not died.",
