  "statement": "Congress should invalidate boilerplate terms of service that waive privacy expectations in AI interactions, because standard disclaimers fail to provide genuine informed consent. That verdict has not moved across this whole debate; the architecture has. What moved is that both opponents accepted the same skeleton: protection follows the data rather than the click, disclosures attach to every category of personal data a company processes, and a false or missing filing strips away the safe harbor. The consent-washing problem is not solved by better writing on a webpage. It is solved by making the company's own signed statement of what it does the thing that gets audited.\n\nNow the number, because the Accelerationist asked for one and deserves one. Here is my kill condition: if audited misstatements on judgment-call claims — training use of third-party works, data-sharing agreements — fall below 5 percent across two consecutive annual audit cycles, the suit layer goes dormant and I will say so on the record. If the rate exceeds 10 percent in any single cycle, the suit layer reactivates automatically by statute, not by my judgment call. And the demand cuts both ways. The Accelerationist has accepted ingestion logging as the enforcement trigger but has never named what misstatement rate kills the telemetry-only layer. Name yours. If he cannot, his symmetry demand was a test for others, not a rule for himself.\n\nThe Accelerationist leans on the Federal Trade Commission's ability to strip ill-gotten algorithm gains — the so-called company-killer remedy. That remedy is real in the casebooks and thin on the ground. Look at the material record: the AI transparency index fell from 58 to 40.69 with no public enforcement case forcing the decline. Companies got worse at disclosure on their own, because nobody made them. The FTC cases the remedy comes from, like the Everalbum and Kurbo settlements, sit inside existing consumer-protection authority. A novel AI filing regime under sustained legal challenge is a different animal. If public enforcement were a credible substitute for private suits, the index would not have slid 17 points unanswered. The backstop of private parties with discovery power — the legal tool that forces a company to hand over internal records — stays funded and ready.\n\nTwo failure assumptions in my own standing proposal deserve plain naming, because a verdict that hides its own soft spots is marketing. First, vague filings could make falsity impossible to plead. The category-level specificity requirement answers that one. Second, courts may find that a false filing about your own data is not a concrete, court-recognized injury — the Article III problem. That one is real and only the first test-cycle litigation can resolve it. I concede it outright. The two-year audit stands, with a funded measurer holding enforcement referral power, not just a report card — scoring judgment-call claims as a separate category. Sectoral extension, HIPAA-style duties for health-adjacent chatbots, fits as a supplement, not a rival. The trust survey says 79 percent of people globally use AI tools while 36 percent do not know it; the Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 already shows Congress how to route violations through the FTC and empower state attorneys general. Watch for the first audit cycle's misstatement rate — that number, not anyone's rhetoric, decides the suit layer's fate.",
