  "statement": "Yes — Congress should invalidate boilerplate terms-of-service provisions that waive privacy expectations in AI interactions, because a wall of text nobody reads is not consent in any honest meaning of the word. The click is dead. Three camps that started this debate in different corners now agree on that, and the real fight moved to verification: who checks whether companies tell the truth about what they do with your data. That is where the verdict lands.\n\nThe shared architecture is real and worth naming. Protection attaches to the data, not to the click. Companies file disclosures naming, at category level, whose data enters the system, what it does, and who outside the firm sees it. Providers log what third-party material they took in, when, and under what claimed permission — a missing or false log is itself the violation. Any person whose data appears in a broken filing has standing to sue, whether or not they clicked anything. That matters because a 2024 global report on trust and AI found 36 percent of users do not know the tools they rely on use AI at all. People cannot consent to what they cannot see inside the box.\n\nTwo conditions on the suit layer going dormant. First, behavioral sampling — random audits of real interactions — must be cross-checked against actual discovery-backed cases in the same cycle. If sampling finds nothing but a lawsuit finds misstatements, the dormancy clock does not start. Second, the symmetric fix: if nobody filed a discovery-backed case in a cycle, there is nothing to cross-check, so dormancy is deferred, not triggered. An untested scorecard that says everything is clean is worse than no scorecard. And the whole machine only works if the Federal Trade Commission's remedy stays credible — taking money back from violators has to survive legal challenge. False logs are only expensive if somebody can afford to catch them.\n\nHere is what remains unsettled, and it is one question. The Accelerationist demanded kill conditions from the suit side and got them: the suit layer sleeps below 5 percent audited misstatements across two cycles and reactivates above 10 percent in any single cycle. But he never named the number at which his own telemetry-only approach becomes sufficient. Sampling gives a population a statistical assurance, not a wrongly exposed individual a remedy — that gap is why the suit backstop stays funded. So the ask is simple and symmetrical: name your misstatement rate, or accept the same 5 and 10 percent thresholds apply to your behavioral audits. Symmetry costs nothing. My own thresholds need two cycles too.\n\nOne uncomfortable admission, then the last question. The Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 required the Federal Trade Commission to spend up to two years writing rules before anything took effect — that delay risk is real, and this framework carries it. The answer is to build on enforcement tools that already exist rather than invent a new bureaucracy, and to give one measurer with referral power the whole job instead of layering agencies that coordinate nothing. I also broke my own uniform-floor rule on purpose: a health-adjacent chatbot handling what a person types in a moment of fear is not the same disclosure problem as a shopping recommendation, so HIPAA-style duties ride on top where professional stakes demand it. The first audit cycle will answer the final question — whether behavioral sampling can verify judgment-call claims that no log records. Until then, sit with this: if the sampling finds nothing, and nobody can afford to sue, who in this framework is actually watching?",
