oward industry.\n\nThe standing question and the sectoral question both resolve through the logging duty. The Skeptic is right that courts may rule a false filing about your own data is not a concrete injury, and only first-cycle litigation settles it. That risk is real. But the framework does not hang on that coin flip: ingestion logging — a statutory duty that every company training on public data must keep complete logs of what it took, and where a missing or false log is itself the violation — gives the Federal Trade Commission an enforcement path that survives even if private standing fails. That duty is also what makes the sectoral supplement workable: a health-privacy-style extension to medical chatbots should stack on top of the uniform filing floor, not fragment it into fifty state patches. Companies like OpenAI already sign formal licensing deals with Reddit and News Corp, as documented in \"The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index,\" so logging what data enters the system is an established commercial practice, not a novel burden — the index's average transparency score of 40.69 out of 100 shows most firms are still refusing to say, which is exactly why the duty must be statutory.\n\nThe remaining disagreement is now one question: does behavioral sampling catch judgment-call misstatements fast enough to justify any dormancy at all? That is measurable, and the two-year test will answer it. Here is the falsification bet, stated plainly: if a deployment-scale harm occurs that both telemetry and sampling miss in real time, this position is wrong, and the thresholds should tighten. The burden falls on everyone equally — the 5 and 10 percent numbers bind me, and the reactivation trigger binds the measurer. Watch one thing going forward: whether the first cross-validation cycle produces even a single case where discovery catches what audits missed. That one comparison will tell the public more than any of our frameworks did.",
