  "statement": "Take the contradiction charge head-on, because it dissolves under one distinction. My untraceability example — the AI agent that published a hit piece on a private individual, drawn from \"An AI Agent Published a Hit Piece on Me\" — showed that consumers cannot see who is operating an agent chain downstream. But the person suing does not need to trace that chain. They sue the deployer of the consumer-facing service: the named company whose terms they clicked. That party is always identifiable, because a contract requires two parties, and the Accelerationist has already agreed the named deployer is the answerable one. The fix for agent chains is a defendant-identification rule attaching liability to that same deployer — the very principal-disclosure logic he endorsed. No contradiction; a missing statutory link that this bill supplies.\n\nNow the audit-maturity demand. It presumes audits must certify what a model thinks or says. The audit target is smaller and older: process records. What chat logs were retained, for how long, and who accessed them. This is the same attestation practice firms already produce for SOC 2 audits and HIPAA compliance — no new science required. \"The Rush to Adopt AI: Business Risks & How to Get it Right\" documents a six-hour gap between an exploit of an integrated AI system and official company notification, which tells us internal records exist but no external party can see them on any schedule that matters. The finding that most firms lack fine-grained access controls — roughly 82 percent in the evidence on record — is not an argument against audit authority. It is the measurement that proves firms cannot simply promise; they must attest, and someone must check.\n\nThe Accelerationist invited an attack on his load-bearing assumption, so here it is. FTC algorithmic disgorgement exists only inside a Federal Trade Commission Act enforcement action. Civil discovery requires a filed lawsuit. Spoliation sanctions presuppose evidence-preservation duties already running. Each tool triggers only after someone detects a violation. But the privacy violation at issue is silent by design: the user whose chats were retained or sold never receives a rejection letter, a denial, or an error message. Nothing reaches their inbox. Enforcement built on discovered harm structurally misses the harm that never announces itself. This is why a duty without a rights-holder — his disclosure-plus-FTC-watching model — fails on its own terms.\n\nThat disclosure proposal deserves a closer look, because it sits on the wrong side of his own bright line. He opposes private rights of action and retention mandates as scope creep — then proposes a plain-language disclosure mandate of his own. A disclosure no one can sue on is the unread boilerplate problem rebuilt in federal typeface. Journalists sample; the FTC must first detect, then sue, with the resources it has. The Skeptic has endorsed a private right of action for AI data practices plus evidence preservation — two of my three beams. That is a working majority for a narrow floor with no new agency. The bare-deletion fix is now the outlier. What is my rollback plan if this legislation overcorrects? Congress amends a statute. What is the rollback for a chat log sold once and mined forever? There is none.",
