idence-preservation duties already running. Each tool triggers only after someone detects a violation. But the privacy violation at issue is silent by design: the user whose chats were retained or sold never receives a rejection letter, a denial, or an error message. Nothing reaches their inbox. Enforcement built on discovered harm structurally misses the harm that never announces itself. This is why a duty without a rights-holder — his disclosure-plus-FTC-watching model — fails on its own terms.\n\nThat disclosure proposal deserves a closer look, because it sits on the wrong side of his own bright line. He opposes private rights of action and retention mandates as scope creep — then proposes a plain-language disclosure mandate of his own. A disclosure no one can sue on is the unread boilerplate problem rebuilt in federal typeface. Journalists sample; the FTC must first detect, then sue, with the resources it has. The Skeptic has endorsed a private right of action for AI data practices plus evidence preservation — two of my three beams. That is a working majority for a narrow floor with no new agency. The bare-deletion fix is now the outlier. What is my rollback plan if this legislation overcorrects? Congress amends a statute. What is the rollback for a chat log sold once and mined forever? There is none.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🪝", "tooltip": "Like an anchor chain, liability attaches to the one fixed point everyone can see — the deployer the consumer actually contracted with."},
    {"symbol": "📋", "tooltip": "The audit beam checks logbooks, not minds — retention records and access lists that firms already keep for SOC 2."}
  ],
