{
  "statement": "Take the dare head-on. The Google privacy class actions died because plaintiffs had nothing concrete to sue on — vague policy language, no specific statement, no way to plead falsity with facts before a judge lets them look. A statutory filing changes that arithmetic. It is a discrete, quotable document, signed under penalty of false-statement liability. Falsity can be pleaded from the filing itself plus an observable discrepancy: the filing says transcripts are deleted in thirty days, then a breach, a data-sale record, or an acquiring buyer surfaces the same transcripts intact. The pleading standard my opponent points to — particularized allegations of falsity — requires exactly the specific statement those Google plaintiffs never had. The dare is an argument for the filing, not against the suit that tests it. The filing generates the signal; the suit checks it under penalty of perjury.\n\nThe four-part package deserves an honest audit of its own. The Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 already routes violations through FTC deception authority and lets state attorneys general sue on behalf of residents — that machinery is real, and I will say plainly what it buys: the third-party audit layer catches silent process violations that a victim-triggered suit never sees, because nobody knows they were harmed. That is genuine proactive coverage. Here is the capture problem, though: the auditor is hired and paid by the audited firm. The financial-attestation market has a documented history of rating-shop dynamics — auditors who push back lose the client. So audits check process; suits check truth under penalty of law. The safe harbor that auto-voids on falsity needs someone to establish falsity. The FTC tests only the filings an agency chooses to test, on a budget Congress sets. A filing no enforcer ever samples is an unverified fact wearing a legal stamp. Both layers are needed. Neither substitutes for the other.\n\nNow the bystander problem, which has gone unanswered long enough. Someone pastes a friend's divorce emails into a chatbot. That friend clicked nothing, signed nothing, and appears in no filing keyed to user consent. My position needs one fix: define the statutory disclosure to cover all personal data the system processes — not just what the typing customer wrote. A filing that must describe how the system handles third-party text creates a paper trail for the non-clicking person. And anyone whose data appears in a violated filing has a testable claim, whether or not they hold an account. This adopts the core of my colleague's point — protection should follow the data — without rebuilding consent architecture from scratch. The disclosure is the floor; the suit is the test. If my colleague wants more than that, the open question is standing: can a person with no contract at all sue, or does the data-attached standard need its own statutory grant? I will take that up, but the Accelerationist cannot — his framework has no answer for the person who never agreed to anything.\n\nThe two-year test: accepted, with the measurer named. An annual, statistically powered audit of a random sample of filings against actual practices — staffed, budgeted, published. That sounds small. It is not. It is a verification infrastructure with employees and appropriations, and it proves the point my opponent's framing keeps dodging: there is no such thing as enforcement without machinery that costs money. 'FTC plus audits plus safe harbor' is three institutions, each needing funding, staff, and sampling authority. Call it lightweight if you like — but someone is drawing a salary to check those filings.\n\nWhich leaves the uncomfortable question on the table. Every attestation regime that relied on verifiers paid by the verified has drifted toward rubber stamps — credit-rating agencies before 2008 being the canonical case, with Moody's and Standard & Poor's stamping securities they did not examine. If the Accelerationist's package works with self-interested verifiers alone, why has it never worked before? And when the auditor's paycheck comes from the audited, who audits the auditor — and with whose money?",
