attempts.raw_response:
I conditionally agree: a rule nobody can invoke is advice, not law, but the standing problem is already solved by the filing mechanism both sides endorsed. My answer is concrete: Congress should grant statutory standing to any person whose personal data is described in a disclosure filing a company violates, whether or not that person ever clicked "agree." The filing is the hook. No contract required.

Here is why that works mechanically. Under the data-attached disclosure we already accepted, a company must file a public statement covering every category of personal data it processes — including text a user pastes about a spouse, a coworker, a client. If the company's actual practices contradict that filing, the contradiction is on paper. The bystander does not need to peer inside anyone's chat. She quotes the filing, points to the mismatch, and pleads falsity on day one. This answers the pleading problem directly: courts throw out cases that require discovery before discovery, but a filed document is evidence before anything is discovered. The Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 already shows the template — a violation there is treated as an unfair or deceptive practice under the FTC Act, and state attorneys general can sue on behalf of residents. Extend that logic one line further: a private right of action for anyone named by the data the filing describes.

Now the parts of the Accelerationist's package I accept. The one-line extension of the filing to all processed data is a genuine fix, and the "false attestation is itself the violation" structure is stronger than anything I proposed — it flips the safe harbor into a trapdoor. I was wrong to call that structure consent-washing. Machine-checkable telemetry also earns real credit: public samplers reading deletion logs and retention timestamps are not the paid-auditor model I attacked. A timestamp is either there or it is not. I accept that layer for exactly those claims.

But telemetry has a hard boundary, and it sits where the money is. A sampler can verify that data was deleted on schedule. It cannot verify how a third party's pasted text was actually used in training, or whether a data-sharing agreement matches what the filing says. Those are judgment calls, and judgment calls made by auditors who are paid by the company they judge repeat the credit-ratings problem — Moody's and S&P stamped mortgage bundles AAA in 2007 because issuers, not investors, paid the fees. Anyone claiming the lawsuit layer is redundant cost has to explain who checks the judgment claims when the auditor's paycheck depends on the answer.

Both sides already agreed to a two-year empirical test with a named measurer. So here is the falsifiable split: if the first cycle of verified filings shows telemetry catching training-use violations without any lawsuit, the suit layer dies and I will bury it. If the filings come back clean while practices stay dirty, the telemetry-only position has to say, on the record, who it thinks is watching. And a question for the Accelerationist: when an auditor catches a falsified filing, whose discovery opens — and does your privacy concern for the bystander survive the answer?

