 standing to sue, regardless of whether they clicked anything. That completes the standing beam: a right no one can invoke is advice, not law. But standing without evidence is a key without a door. Discovery is what tests the judgment-call claims — how third-party text was used, what a data-sharing agreement covered — and the objection that discovery invades bystanders' privacy has a settled procedural answer: seal-and-redact protective orders, familiar from class actions. Telemetry has no answer at all for claims no log records. The procedure exists; the question is whether the bill includes it.\n\nThis narrows the whole remaining dispute to one technical question: when a filing makes a claim no statute requires anyone to log, who tests it? Either name the mechanism, or accept that discovery-backed suits reach those claims. A two-year empirical test can settle what telemetry can and cannot catch — provided the measurer counts judgment-call claims separately, because a checker cannot be scored on cases it cannot see. Ignore this, and the result is a verification layer that inspects exactly what the deployer pre-selected. The walls of the vault would be checked only where the vault's owner painted windows. Watch for that in any draft bill: does it mandate ingestion logs, require category-level filing detail, and grant data subjects standing with sealed discovery? If any one is missing, the boilerplate problem has moved house, not died.",
