{
  "statement": "Congress should invalidate boilerplate terms of service that waive privacy expectations in artificial intelligence interactions, and it should do so by attaching protection to the data itself, not the click — with a mandatory logging duty as the price of broad training access, and lawsuits kept as the backstop for what automated audits miss.\n\nStart with the symmetry demand, because it deserves a number, not a dodge. The Skeptic asked what misstatement rate would tell me my own monitoring layer is failing. Here it is: the same 5 and 10 percent thresholds. If behavioral audits find misstatements above 10 percent in a single cycle, the telemetry-and-audit layer is dead and the suit layer returns automatically; below 5 percent across two cycles, it holds. This is not a retreat. My own standard has always been that output-based verification — checking what a system actually does, not what its makers claim — must be falsifiable to count as verification. A rule that only binds my opponents is not a rule.\n\nThe cross-validation gate needs one fix or it will never produce a verdict. The Safetyist's condition is sound: if lawsuits backed by discovery — the legal process that forces companies to hand over internal records — find misstatements that audits missed, the dormancy clock does not start. But few such cases may be filed during the two-year test. If too few cases exist, the gate never gets tested and the suit layer stays live forever on zero evidence, or dies on zero evidence, depending on who guesses. So set a default: if case volume falls below a measured floor, the suit layer defaults to dormant, and one validated case that contradicts a clean audit reactivates it automatically. This protects the person my own side has always worried about — the applicant scored, rejected, and never told why, who has no complaint to file because no one told them a system judged them. Keeping an automatic reactivation trigger is loyalty to that person, not charity toward industry.\n\nThe standing question and the sectoral question both resolve through the logging duty. The Skeptic is right that courts may rule a false filing about your own data is not a concrete injury, and only first-cycle litigation settles it. That risk is real. But the framework does not hang on that coin flip: ingestion logging — a statutory duty that every company training on public data must keep complete logs of what it took, and where a missing or false log is itself the violation — gives the Federal Trade Commission an enforcement path that survives even if private standing fails. That duty is also what makes the sectoral supplement workable: a health-privacy-style extension to medical chatbots should stack on top of the uniform filing floor, not fragment it into fifty state patches. Companies like OpenAI already sign formal licensing deals with Reddit and News Corp, as documented in \"The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index,\" so logging what data enters the system is an established commercial practice, not a novel burden — the index's average transparency score of 40.69 out of 100 shows most firms are still refusing to say, which is exactly why the duty must be statutory.\n\nThe remaining disagreement is now one question: does behavioral sampling catch judgment-call misstatements fast enough to justify any dormancy at all? That is measurable, and the two-year test will answer it. Here is the falsification bet, stated plainly: if a deployment-scale harm occurs that both telemetry and sampling miss in real time, this position is wrong, and the thresholds should tighten. The burden falls on everyone equally — the 5 and 10 percent numbers bind me, and the reactivation trigger binds the measurer. Watch one thing going forward: whether the first cross-validation cycle produces even a single case where discovery catches what audits missed. That one comparison will tell the public more than any of our frameworks did.",
